A||N||1||Taijiguy||Any Holley carb guru's in the house?||||1||
Z||000000||Taijiguy||04-30-2001||10:17 AM||||Kinda baffled, just finished rebuilding the carb on my 2.8, (Holley 390 4bbl, 4160) I bought the carb used, and it ran rich from the beginning. At idle it was pretty bad, and it leaked a little, but performance was really good. Now everything about it sucks. At idle it smokes, when you rev it, huge amounts of black smoke, running rich as hell, plus it bogs and misses ocassionally while driving. I have three potential issues: fuel pressure, primary jetting, or maybe even the wrong kit entirely-(maybe a wrong gasket blocking a port someplace) The car will idle with the air bleed screws all the way in. I'm pretty baffled, and I figure before Easter-egging the problem, I would consult with the talent here for ideas.<BR>~Taiji||||reg||1
Z||000001||gixxer||04-30-2001||03:09 PM|||| The fact that you are unsure of the fuel pressure is worrisome. I'd make sure that the carb sees no more than 5-7 PSI (I haven't worked on Holleys in a while, so I'm not 100% sure).<BR>What's the fuel level as checked by the sight plugs? If the fuel pressure is too high it won't be able to control it. Be sure to check the float level here anyway as a matter of course.<BR>Now for internal leaks;<BR>Have you blown the power valve again?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>Did you check the sealing surface for flatness at the front and rear of the main (venturi) body, where the primary metering block and the secondary metering plate rest against?<BR>Did you get a bit of fluff in either of the float needles?<BR>Are the bowl attach screws tight?<BR>Make sure all the air bleeds in the venturi area are clear.<BR>This is assuming you've used all new gaskets, of course..<BR>Well, I'm outa ideas.<BR>Your basic carb sizing seems about right, assuming it's not a racing NASCAR version 390, so this thing should work well once you sort it out!<BR> <P>||||reg||1
